## Proof

**Step 1: Domain analysis.** For $f(x)$ to be defined for all real $x$, the integrand $g(t) = \left|\log_2(\log_3(\log_4(\cos t + a)))\right|$ must be defined for all $t \in \mathbb{R}$. Tracing the nested logarithms inward to outward:

**(i)** $\log_4(\cos t + a)$ requires $\cos t + a > 0$ for all $t$. Since $\min_t(\cos t + a) = a - 1$, we need $a - 1 > 0$, i.e., $a > 1$.

**(ii)** $\log_3(\log_4(\cos t + a))$ requires $\log_4(\cos t + a) > 0$ for all $t$, i.e., $\cos t + a > 4^0 = 1$ for all $t$. This gives $a - 1 > 1$, i.e., $a > 2$.

**(iii)** $\log_2(\log_3(\log_4(\cos t + a)))$ requires $\log_3(\log_4(\cos t + a)) > 0$ for all $t$, i.e., $\log_4(\cos t + a) > 3^0 = 1$ for all $t$, i.e., $\cos t + a > 4^1 = 4$ for all $t$. This gives $a - 1 > 4$, i.e., $a > 5$.

Combining (i)–(iii), the integrand is defined for all real $t$ if and only if $a > 5$.

**Step 2: Monotonicity.** By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, for $a > 5$ the integrand $g$ is continuous, and
$$f'(x) = g(x) = \left|\log_2(\log_3(\log_4(\cos x + a)))\right| \geq 0 \quad \text{for all } x \in \mathbb{R}.$$
So $f$ is non-decreasing. To confirm $f$ is **strictly** increasing, we check that $g$ is not identically zero on any interval.

$g(x) = 0$ iff $\log_2(\log_3(\log_4(\cos x + a))) = 0$, i.e., $\log_3(\log_4(\cos x + a)) = 1$, i.e., $\log_4(\cos x + a) = 3$, i.e., $\cos x + a = 64$, i.e., $\cos x = 64 - a$. Since $\cos x$ is not constant on any interval, $g$ cannot vanish identically on any interval. Hence $f$ is strictly increasing.

**Step 3: Boundary check at $a = 5$.** When $a = 5$, at $t = \pi$ we have $\cos t + a = -1 + 5 = 4$, so $\log_4(4) = 1$, $\log_3(1) = 0$, and $\log_2(0)$ is undefined. Thus $a = 5$ fails. For $a < 5$, the innermost condition (iii) is violated at $t = \pi$ (and nearby), so $f$ is not even defined on all of $\mathbb{R}$.

**Conclusion.** The function $f$ is defined and increasing on all of $\mathbb{R}$ if and only if $a > 5$.

$$\boxed{a \in (5, +\infty)}$$

